<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Nuevo Santander</h1><p class="st-meta">1748 CE – 1821 CE · capital Santander; later San Carlos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Spanish frontier province on the Gulf of Mexico, colonised in a planned campaign by José de Escandón, that became the Mexican state of Tamaulipas and southern Texas.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Gulf coast north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tampico">Tampico</a> was held by independent peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuiltecan">Coahuiltecans</a> and resisted Spanish settlement for two centuries. In 1746 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a> commissioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-escandon">José de Escandón</a> to colonise it, and from 1748 he founded over twenty towns and missions between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/panuco-river">Pánuco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueces-river">Nueces</a> rivers, including Camargo, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reynosa">Reynosa</a> and, in 1755, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laredo">Laredo</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-escandon">Escandón</a> governed as colonist and military commander, granting land to settler families from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a> and Querétaro who raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, sheep and horses on large ranchos. The towns were small, defended by soldier-settlers, and missions tried to convert local hunter-gatherers, many of whom died or were displaced. The province formed part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain</a> and at times came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commandancy-general-of-the-internal-provinces">Commandancy General of the Internal Provinces</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>With Mexican independence in 1821 the province passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mexico">Mexico</a> and in 1824 became the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a>. The lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-grande">Rio Grande</a>, disputed after Texan independence, went to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a> in 1848; the ranching culture of the region shaped the later cowboy and vaquero tradition.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/nuevo-santander.jpg" alt="Nuevo Santander" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Ningyou. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Cross_of_Burgundy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1748 CE – 1821 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Santander; later San Carlos</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>José de Escandón</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a>, <a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Colony of Nuevo Santander</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1784&amp;focus=nuevo-santander" aria-label="Nuevo Santander on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1920 1183 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Nuevo Santander lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2240 1439L2321 1717M2240 1439L2142 1327"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2321 1717h0M2142 1327h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2240 1439h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2240 1439h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its capital. <a href="/atlas?year=1784&amp;focus=nuevo-santander">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

